A heavy flying animal needs a strong launch. Birds usually jump with their legs. But pterosaurs walked on all four limbs, with their wings folded up. In 2008, scientist Michael Habib suggested they used their powerful arms to vault into the air, like a pole vaulter, then flapped hard to climb.

Once airborne, Quetzalcoatlus probably soared on rising warm air, much as vultures and eagles do today, saving energy. Its wings were made of skin stiffened with fibres, stretched from one enormously long finger back to the legs. Muscles and blood vessels inside the wing let it change the wing's shape in flight. Some scientists think it could have covered huge distances in a single flight, though nobody can be sure.

{{story:how-birds-fly}}

An otter's body is shaped like a torpedo with legs. Its feet are webbed, and its thick tail works as a rudder. When it dives, it can close its ears and nostrils tight. Two layers of fur trap a blanket of air next to the skin, so the otter stays dry underneath even after an hour of swimming.

The best tool is on its face. Stiff whiskers pick up tiny ripples made by a moving fish, which helps in brown, muddy rivers where eyes are almost useless. Small-clawed otters hunt differently. Their claws are tiny and their fingers are nimble, so they feel under stones for crabs and snails, almost like you searching for a lost coin under a sofa.

{{story:sea-otters}}

The shell is usually tough plastic. On a bicycle helmet it is thin and light, while a motorcycle helmet has a thick shell to resist sharp objects and high-speed slides. The liner is the crushable foam. Comfort pads stop it rubbing your skin, but they do almost nothing in a crash.

The strap may be the most ignored part. An unfastened helmet can fly off before your head hits the ground. A good fit means two fingers between the strap and your chin, and the front edge sitting about two fingers above your eyebrows.

{{story:road-safety}}

In the 1500s, the poet-composer Purandara Dasa created a set of graded lessons for Carnatic music. Students still start with them today, which is why he is called the father of Carnatic music.

Around 200 years ago, three composers known as the Trinity, Tyagaraja, Muthuswami Dikshitar and Syama Sastri, wrote thousands of songs called *kritis*. A Carnatic concert is mostly built around such songs. Singers add their own improvised passages, but the composition stays at the heart. Each January, musicians gather at Tyagaraja's resting place in Thiruvaiyaru to sing his songs together.

{{story:akbar}}

The tower was made from more than 18,000 iron pieces. Each was designed on paper first, down to the millimetre, then made in Eiffel's factory outside Paris. On site, workers fitted them together like a giant construction set, fixing them with about 2.5 million rivets, hammered in while red-hot.

Building took just over two years, from January 1887 to March 1889. About 300 workers did the job. The open iron lattice, full of triangles, makes the tower strong while letting wind blow straight through. That's partly why it is so light for its size.

{{story:rust}}

Biles is about 1.42 metres tall, roughly the height of an average ten-year-old. A small body is easier to spin quickly, a bit like a skater who pulls in her arms to twirl faster. But her real secret is power. She jumps higher than most gymnasts, which gives her more time in the air to twist.

In gymnastics, each skill has a difficulty value. When a gymnast is the first to perform a new skill at a major competition, it can be named after her. Biles has skills named after her on the floor, the vault and the beam, including a triple-twisting double backflip.

{{story:gravity-fall}}

In 1917, the Japanese eye doctor Shinobu Ishihara published plates covered in coloured dots. Hidden among them is a number made of dots of a slightly different colour. Most people see it at once. Someone with red-green colour blindness may see a different number, or none.

Versions of the Ishihara test are still used in schools and clinics worldwide. Some jobs, such as flying planes or driving trains, need good colour vision, so people applying for them are tested.

{{story:traffic-lights}}

Human brains are good at spotting problems. For our ancestors, noticing a snake mattered more than noticing a pretty flower. So bad things tend to stick in memory, and good things slide past.

Gratitude may work by pushing back against that habit. Writing down good moments makes you pause on them. It is a bit like [[@mindfulness|mindfulness]], which trains attention. Thanking people also strengthens friendships, and close friends are one of the most reliable sources of [[@happiness|happiness]] that researchers have found.

{{story:happiness}}

After a very loud event, such as a concert or a festival night full of [[@fireworks|fireworks]], many people notice their hearing seems muffled, or they hear ringing. The ringing is called tinnitus. It often fades within a day as stressed hair cells recover.

But repeated damage adds up. Each time, a few more hair cells may die. Over years, people slowly lose the ability to hear high sounds, like birdsong or some consonants in speech. Conversations in noisy rooms become hard to follow.

{{story:beethoven}}

The most common heart problems in adults start inside the arteries. Over many years, fatty material called plaque can build up in their walls, making them narrower and stiffer. If an artery feeding the heart muscle gets blocked, part of the heart is starved of oxygen. That is a heart attack. A blocked artery in the brain causes a stroke.

The World Health Organization says diseases of the heart and blood vessels kill more people than any other cause worldwide. Many of those deaths are linked to things people can change, such as smoking, inactivity, unhealthy food and high blood pressure.

{{story:salt}}

In the late 1980s, engineers at companies including Xerox and Apple started working on a single code for all languages. The first version of Unicode came out in 1991.

Underneath, each number is stored as [[@binary-code|binary code]], the 0s and 1s of computer memory. A clever system called UTF-8 stores common English letters in one byte and others in two, three or four. It became so popular that almost every website uses it today. Each character also has an official name in capitals, such as DEVANAGARI LETTER KA, and a code written in a counting system called hexadecimal, so क appears in Unicode's charts as U+0915.

{{story:binary-code}}

Look closely at the biggest pillars and you see arms carved down their sides, with long fingers meeting over a belt. Some have a fox-skin loincloth. The T-shape is a head and body. These are figures, perhaps gods, ancestors or spirits, standing in a ring.

The animals are mostly dangerous ones: snakes, scorpions, wild boars, big cats and vultures. One famous pillar, called the Vulture Stone, shows a vulture with a round object above its wing. Some researchers say it records a comet or the sky. Most archaeologists are not convinced.

{{story:stone-age}}

Gandhi was 60 years old. He chose marchers from his ashram carefully, from many parts of India and different faiths. They walked through the spring heat, about 16 km a day, stopping in villages.

At each village, Gandhi spoke to crowds about the salt tax, cleanliness, [[@khadi|khadi]] and Hindu–Muslim unity. Thousands joined parts of the walk. Reporters from India and abroad followed, sending stories and photographs around the world. By the time the marchers reached Dandi, the whole country was watching.

{{story:salt}}

When a country argues over which big city should lead, building a new one can settle it. In 1790, the [[@congress-usa|US Congress]] agreed to create a new capital on the Potomac River, between the northern and southern states. Washington, DC, was laid out with wide avenues, and the government moved there in 1800.

Australia had the same problem. Sydney and Melbourne both wanted to be the capital, so the government built Canberra between them. It was founded in 1913. Brazil went further. In 1960, it moved its capital from Rio de Janeiro to Brasília, a futuristic new city built in just a few years in the middle of the country.

{{story:government-how}}

The word "Quran" means "the recitation". It was first shared aloud, and it is still meant to be heard. Reciters train for years to chant it with exact pronunciation and rhythm, following rules called *tajwid*.

A person who has memorised the whole book is called a *hafiz*. Many children in India, Pakistan, Indonesia, Egypt and elsewhere begin learning as young as six or seven, often finishing in a few years. During the holy month of Ramadan, many Muslims try to read or hear the entire Quran, and mosques hold special night prayers where it is recited in parts.

{{story:calligraphy-world}}
